# Atlas design

## Source of truth (mandatory)

- **Approved Figma is the visual source of truth.** If implementation and Figma disagree on visuals, Figma wins.
- Canonical file: Atlas Design System (see Build Manifest for file key and frozen page list).
- **Atlas Design v1.0 is frozen.** Do not redesign frozen pages during implementation.
- **Editorial Publishing System (Figma Page 26) is frozen.** Reuse approved patterns; do not invent parallel publishing chrome.

## No design drift (mandatory)

- Implement approved compositions — do not “improve” spacing, type, color, or layout ad hoc.
- Responsive behavior must match Figma recomposition at **1440 / 768 / 390**, not stacked desktop.
- Design changes require **review and approval before implementation**. Update Figma (and Manifest notes) first when a change is intentional.
- Temporary placeholders are allowed only when tracked in an explicit asset inventory before launch.

## Visual direction (mandatory)

Atlas must feel: modern, distinctive, editorial, technical, premium, restrained, human, memorable, confident without arrogance, and current without trend-chasing.

## Prohibited defaults (mandatory)

Do not default to:

- Generic SaaS dashboard styling or off-the-shelf Tailwind portfolio templates
- Excessive glassmorphism, neon cyberpunk, animated particles
- Decorative dot grids everywhere, glowing borders on every component
- Gradient blobs, floating abstract shapes, random 3D cubes
- Large empty heroes with no meaningful visual content
- Excessive scroll effects or motion added only to impress
- AI-generated imagery as generic filler
- Repetitive rounded cards with identical visual weight

## Image strategy (mandatory)

Atlas **cannot** rely only on colors, gradients, lines, and geometric shapes.

Prefer meaningful imagery: real product screenshots, UI close-ups, device compositions, project galleries, before/after, architecture/workflow diagrams, sparingly used code/terminal imagery, professional photography where appropriate, editorial article imagery, and project-specific artwork only when it adds value.

Every image must communicate information, demonstrate real work, establish credibility, add meaningful atmosphere, or improve comprehension. Do not use decorative imagery merely to fill space.

## Layout system (mandatory)

Require: strong hierarchy, intentional whitespace, editorial composition, sophisticated responsive behavior, clear rhythm/alignment, consistent spacing, purposeful asymmetry where appropriate, distinctive project presentation, carefully controlled accents, excellent typography, accessible contrast, visible focus states, and `prefers-reduced-motion` support.

Motion must communicate state or reinforce hierarchy — not decorate.

**The application owns layout. Strapi provides content, not arbitrary page design.**

Visual QA and a11y gates for milestone completion: `atlas-definition-of-done`. Token/system docs: `atlas-documentation`.
